WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:07.360
SETH VARGO: Hi, everyone,
and welcome to Secrets

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:09.800
in Serverless 2.0.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:13.830
I'm Seth, and I'm a developer
advocate at Google Cloud.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.900
In the past, I've
spoke about strategies

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.330
for managing secrets in
serverless environments,

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.620
but a lot has
changed since then.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.440
So let's dive right in.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.770
If you've seen the earlier
versions of this session,

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:29.070
you're already familiar with
this application architecture,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:32.189
but if you haven't let's
run through it very quickly.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:33.870
Suppose we have a
web application that

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.200
tracks the number of
visitors via a hit counter,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.710
like a 1990 style
website at its finest.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:44.370
We only want to pay while
our site is being accessed,

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.140
so we're going to
leverage Google Cloud

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.780
Run and its ability to scale
to zero for this application.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.640
But because our application
can scale up and down

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.890
we need to persist
our visitor counter

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.490
in some external persistent
data store, like Redis.

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:04.580
But our application needs to
authenticate and communicate

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:05.870
with Redis.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:09.410
So that begs the question,
how do we securely

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:14.960
provide the Redis password to
our serverless application?

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.920
That's the journey that we'll
be going on together today.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.860
We'll look at three unique
and independent approaches

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.410
to managing secrets in
serverless environments.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:26.680
The first, is via plaintext
environment variables.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.858
Then, encrypted
environment variables.

00:01:28.858 --> 00:01:31.150
And then finally, with a
centralized secrets management

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.050
solution.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:34.940
Let's jump right into the first.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.580
Plaintext environment variables.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:40.040
Plaintext environment
variables are, by far,

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.260
the simplest architecture.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.390
We create a Redis
password, and we

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.060
deploy it directly into
our serverless application

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.620
by an environment variable.

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:53.240
Let's take a look at a
demo of how this works.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.400
All right, let's take a
look at our first example.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.290
We're going to
deploy an application

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:01.160
to Cloud Run that puts secrets
in environment variables,

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:03.210
in plaintext.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.117
Let's take a look at
what this looks like.

00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:06.700
What we're going to
do, is we're going

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.250
to look at our deployment
script, which I wrote to just

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:12.530
clean things up a little bit.

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:15.690
We're collecting the IP
address of our Redis instance,

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:19.040
and we're getting the Redis
password, again in plaintext.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:21.860
Then we're going to build our
application as a container,

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:25.225
push it to a Docker registry,
and deploy it to Cloud Run.

00:02:25.225 --> 00:02:26.600
And you'll also
notice that we're

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.300
using the Redis password
here, via an environment

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:32.450
variable to Cloud Run.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:38.360
Let's go ahead and quit, and
let's deploy our application.

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:39.860
So what this is
going to do, is this

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:44.210
is going to build a container,
push a container to our Google

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.950
Container Registry,
and then it's

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:49.250
going to ask Cloud Run
to deploy that container

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.320
with those
environment variables.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.870
Our application will read
those environment variables

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.940
at start time, and deploy them.

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:00.740
So we are pushing to
container registry now,

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:03.230
and then we're waiting
for our Cloud Run

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:06.770
to pull that image and
route traffic to it.

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.810
And when this is done,
we will get a URL

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.370
where we can visit our service.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:18.317
Great.

00:03:18.317 --> 00:03:20.775
So I'm going to go ahead and
open up this URL in a browser.

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.430
Great, here it is.

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.730
And as you can see, this
is a very basic website

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:33.130
that has a hit counter
that's backed by Redis.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:36.190
Every time I visit the site,
I increase the hit counter.

00:03:36.190 --> 00:03:38.500
I can reset the counter.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:40.720
But what happens if
someone breaks the site?

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.510
So you'll notice the reset
counter method actually

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:45.940
includes the count
that I can set to,

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.760
and I can set this
to a negative number

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.130
and the site
continues to function.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:54.610
But what happens if I set it
to something like a fruit?

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.320
Well then my whole
application crashes,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.098
and we're presented this very
nice, helpful debugging page,

00:03:59.098 --> 00:04:00.640
that's very similar
to what you would

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.090
see in like Rails, or Django,
or any popular web framework.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:06.220
We get a bunch of information
about the request,

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:07.732
but we also get
the environment--

00:04:07.732 --> 00:04:09.940
and you'll notice in this
environment our Redis host,

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:14.980
and our Redis password are
available in plaintext.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:17.290
Anyone with access
to this application--

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.899
you can visit this URL
right now in your browser--

00:04:19.899 --> 00:04:22.000
you would have access
to this Redis host,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.628
and this Redis password.

00:04:24.628 --> 00:04:26.920
Hopefully this makes it clear
why plaintext environment

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:29.800
variables aren't a great idea.

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:33.560
Great, let's jump back
over to the slides.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.700
Hopefully that made it really
clear that you should not

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.880
store secrets in plaintext
environment variables,

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:42.640
not only do you open yourself
up to classifications

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.400
of vulnerabilities, it's
really not a best practice.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.970
So that really only leaves
the other two options--

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:52.120
encrypted environment variables,
or a centralized secrets

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.810
management solution.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:58.130
Let's take a look at encrypted
environment variables.

00:04:58.130 --> 00:05:00.400
So instead of taking
our plaintext secret,

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.710
and putting it in an
environment variable,

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:06.100
we can instead encrypt
that secret first.

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:09.130
What that looks like,
is a human or a machine

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.440
takes the plaintext
secret, and they

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.480
give it to a key
management system,

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.970
like Google Cloud Camus.

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:18.790
Google Cloud Camus encrypts
that plaintext data,

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:23.780
and produces what we call cipher
text, or the encrypted secret.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:27.020
We then provide this encrypted
secret as an environment

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:30.140
variable to our
application, this way

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:33.350
the environment variable
has an encrypted secret, not

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:36.000
a plaintext secret.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.700
When our application boots it
communicates with Google Cloud

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:43.800
KMS to decrypt the value
and then get the plaintext,

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.680
but that plaintext
only exists in memory,

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:48.990
it doesn't exist
in the environment

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:52.340
given to the
serverless application.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:55.200
Let's go ahead and
take a look at a demo.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:55.700
Great.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.610
Our second example is going
to leverage Google Cloud KMS

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:00.650
to encrypt the secret
value before we put it

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:04.130
in an environment variable,
then when our application boots,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.920
it will decrypt the
value using Cloud KMS,

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:11.610
and only store the decrypted
secret in plaintext in memory.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.197
So if someone gains
access to the application,

00:06:14.197 --> 00:06:15.780
or the environment
of the application,

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:19.490
they would only see the cipher
text for the encrypted secret.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:21.240
Let's take a look at
what that looks like.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:22.615
The first thing
we have to do, is

00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:25.130
we have to update our
application I've already

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:27.600
written some helper code
that makes this easy.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.720
So we're going to leverage
this KMS decrypt method that

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:36.710
accepts the cipher text,
or the encrypted secret,

00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:41.180
via the Redis pass
environment variable.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:43.370
And then we actually have
to encrypt our secret,

00:06:43.370 --> 00:06:45.750
so let's go ahead and do that.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.180
We're going to run
encrypt string,

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.555
and this is going to encrypt
the string that we give it--

00:06:49.555 --> 00:06:52.430
which in this example
is the Redis password--

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:54.830
with a Google Cloud KMS key.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:57.450
I'm going to copy
this to my clipboard,

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.740
and then we also have to
update our deployment script,

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:03.260
because instead of passing
in the plaintext secret,

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:04.870
we now need to
pass in this cipher

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:08.078
text, or this encrypted secret.

00:07:08.078 --> 00:07:09.620
Notice that we're
still passing in it

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.620
as an environment variable,
but we're passing it

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:12.530
in as the ciphertext.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.215
Now let's go ahead and actually
deploy the application.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.200
And again, this is going to
build and push our container,

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.950
and then we're going
to wait for Cloud Run

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:29.290
to deploy this new revision.

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:31.000
And then after the
revision is deployed,

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.220
the application should
behave exactly the same,

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:35.710
because we only decrypt
the secret at start time,

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.110
and we only eat the
additional cost of decrypting

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:39.970
that secret one time.

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:42.263
Let's go ahead and
jump back over.

00:07:42.263 --> 00:07:44.180
So you'll notice that
the application is still

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:48.250
erroring, because we haven't
fixed the bad value in Redis,

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:52.150
but the Redis password
is now that ciphertext.

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:55.270
And because Google Cloud
Camus is guarded by IAM,

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:58.510
Identity and Access Management,
you cannot connect to this

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:01.510
Redis host using
this Redis password,

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:04.540
because you cannot decrypt
it, unless you have access

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:07.680
to the Cloud Camus KMS key.

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:10.050
Now this is great, but
there are some disadvantages

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:14.100
to this approach, which
we'll talk about in a second.

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:17.390
Great, let's jump back
over to the slides.

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:20.440
So as you can see, encrypted
environment variables

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.690
do protect against
the immediate threat,

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:24.550
and they're a great
starting point,

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:26.840
but there are some drawbacks.

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.720
The biggest of which, is once
you deploy your serverless

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.080
application with an
encrypted secret,

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:34.570
you lose centralized
auditing and visibility

00:08:34.570 --> 00:08:37.059
into how that
secret's being used.

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:39.760
If that's a requirement for
your organization or security

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:41.559
practices, we need
to take a look

00:08:41.559 --> 00:08:44.770
at the third option, which is a
centralized secrets management

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.210
solution.

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:52.030
In this model, the application
requests its secrets directly

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:54.530
from a secrets
management system.

00:08:54.530 --> 00:08:56.800
So as a secret
admin or developer,

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.810
I put my secret into the
centralized storage system,

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:02.980
and as an application, I
request that secret directly

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:05.120
from the storage system.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:07.250
That centralized
storage system is

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:10.340
responsible for encryption,
decryption, logging,

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:14.520
and monitoring,
all in one place.

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:18.240
On Google Cloud our first party
secrets management solution

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:19.500
is Secret Manager.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:22.830
We aim to be that centralized
secret store that provides you

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:26.440
that value of auditing and
logging and visibility.

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:29.980
Google's Secrets Manager, is a
new fully managed GCP product

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:33.250
that provides a secure
and convenient method

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:36.850
for storing API key,
certificates, passwords,

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:38.770
and other sensitive
data that you

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:42.330
might need in your
applications and services.

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:45.360
Secrets rarely differ
across Cloud regions.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.970
For example your
Twilio API key is

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:51.300
unlikely to change if you
move your app from US east one

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:53.160
to Europe west two.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:56.580
For this reason, secret names
in Google Secret Manager

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:59.730
are project global resources.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:02.370
The resource URI,
in this example,

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:05.820
refers to the secret named, my
secret, in the project named,

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.590
my project.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:10.090
Even though that
secret name is global,

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:13.860
the underlying secret
payload, the actual API key,

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.900
is still stored regionally.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:17.370
For example in
this slide you can

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:21.110
see that it's stored in US east
one, US west one, and Europe

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:22.650
north one.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.710
But how does the secret data
end up in those regions?

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.960
Google Cloud handles it all.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.660
We do this with
replication policies,

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:33.550
and we have two replication
policies for Google Secret

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.280
Manager.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.530
For customers who don't
really have a preference where

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.570
their secret
payloads are stored,

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:42.870
you should choose an
automatic replication policy.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:45.030
With this policy, there
is no configuration

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:48.510
and Google decides the best
place to put your secret.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:50.870
But if you're a
customer that has

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:54.230
a requirement that your data
exists only in certain regions,

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:57.080
you should choose a user
managed replication policy,

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:59.780
and pick the specific region
or regions in which you

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:01.910
want the secret to be stored.

00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:04.940
Regardless of which
policy you choose though,

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:06.740
Google Cloud handles
the replication

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:09.620
of that secret across all
of the selected regions.

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:11.420
You don't have to
run a Cloud Function,

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:13.580
or kick off a nightly cron job.

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:15.590
Google is the first
public Cloud provider

00:11:15.590 --> 00:11:19.325
to offer a fully managed secret
replication across regions.

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:25.410
Now I have to admit,
I've been lying.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.140
Up until this point I keep
talking about secrets,

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:30.930
but I'm actually talking
about secret versions.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.120
That's because all secrets
and Secrets Manager

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:35.910
are versioned by
default. The secret

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:37.770
is actually just a
very thin wrapper

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:40.530
around a logical
collection of versions.

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:42.900
The secret version is
what actually stores

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:46.280
the payload data and state.

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:49.790
In your applications you can
pin to a specific version,

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:52.310
or you can use the
latest alias to pick up

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:55.620
the most recent highest
number numbered version.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:58.620
Note, that latest
is a floating alias,

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.720
so if a newer
version is created,

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:03.480
latest automatically
points to that new version.

00:12:06.110 --> 00:12:08.990
Seeker manager uses
an API for its design,

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:11.480
so that means that anything
you can do by clicking buttons

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:14.960
in the Cloud console,
or by running commands

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:18.230
in your terminal
via G Cloud, is also

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:21.510
possible in your favorite
programming language.

00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:26.510
In fact, we have samples in
seven different languages that

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:29.270
allow you to call and execute
Secrets Manager directly

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:31.280
from your serverless
applications.

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:37.280
You can do so in Ruby, or
Python, Java, and .Net,

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:39.200
or PHP Go, and Node js.

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:45.110
Before moving on, you might
be a little bit confused

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:48.860
about Secret Manager
and key manager.

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:51.980
We talked earlier-- and the
second example we looked at--

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:55.050
leveraged Google Cloud KMS.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:57.700
So what is the difference
between Google Cloud

00:12:57.700 --> 00:13:01.320
KMS and Google Secret Manager?

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:04.800
Google Cloud KMS is a
cryptographic Oracle system,

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:07.740
nobody including
yourself or Google,

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:10.910
can extract the
keys in that system.

00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:13.760
Google Cloud KMS
provides an API that

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:16.820
does high level operations,
like encrypt, decrypt, sign,

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:20.620
and verify using those keys.

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:22.580
Secrets Manager,
on the other hand,

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:25.570
is a data storage system
that saves, and allows

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:28.210
you to access secret material.

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:31.540
So while they both involve
encryption and decryption,

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:33.940
Secret Manager is more
of a storage solution,

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:37.840
whereas Cloud KMS is
more of a Crypto Oracle.

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:40.198
Let's take a look at the
different user journeys,

00:13:40.198 --> 00:13:42.490
to help you understand the
difference between these two

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:44.060
products.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:47.250
With Google Cloud KMS, in
order to persist a secret,

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.310
we have to create
an encryption key,

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:51.380
encrypt the secret
with that key,

00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:54.160
and then store the resulting
ciphertext somewhere--

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:57.020
a file system, a
database, or in memory.

00:13:57.020 --> 00:13:59.090
As we saw in the
second example, we

00:13:59.090 --> 00:14:02.020
had to store it in an
environment variable.

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:04.540
Google Cloud KMS does not
store the secret, that's

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:07.350
your responsibility,
and it also doesn't

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:10.200
keep track of how the
secret is used after it's

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:13.500
been encrypted-- because
Google Cloud KMS is concerned

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:16.410
about the encryption keys,
not the actual data that's

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.710
encrypted or decrypted.

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:22.160
With Secret Manager, when you
persist a secret with Secret

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:25.520
Manager, you give it a
name like Twilio API Key,

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:27.980
and you give it the value
that you want to store.

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:30.260
Secret Manager handles the
encryption and decryption

00:14:30.260 --> 00:14:32.330
automatically, as well
as the replication

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:35.310
across regions for ability.

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:38.890
When you want that secret
back with Google Cloud KMS,

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:42.510
you have to provide Google
Cloud KMS the cipher text,

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:45.060
and then Google Cloud KMS
will decrypt that cipher text

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:47.280
and return the plaintext
value, assuming

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.570
you have the proper
authorizations to do so.

00:14:50.570 --> 00:14:52.730
To retrieve a secret
with Secret Manager,

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:54.410
you just access
the secret directly

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:57.440
by name-- example,
Twilio API Key.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:59.510
If you're authorized
to access the secret,

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:04.160
Secret Manager will return
the secret and its metadata.

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:06.480
Let's go ahead and take a
look at using Google Secret

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:09.020
Manager in action.

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:12.320
Our third approach is to use
Google Cloud Secret Manager.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:14.990
Unlike Google Cloud KMS,
Google's Secret Manager

00:15:14.990 --> 00:15:17.480
actually stores the
secret material,

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:20.180
and you only need a
reference to retrieve it.

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:22.500
Let's take a look at
what this looks like.

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:25.500
Again, just like before, we
need to update our application.

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:29.030
So instead of pulling
the secret from KMS,

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:35.290
we're instead going to pull
the secret from Secret Manager,

00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:37.300
using this access
secret function.

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:38.800
And like I said
before, this code is

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:40.300
all open source on
GitHub, and there'll

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:42.310
be a link in the slides,
and in the show notes,

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:44.720
so you could always
take a look later.

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:47.400
Let's go ahead and save this.

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:49.420
And now we need to
actually create the secret.

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:51.670
I've written a little script
called Create Secret that

00:15:51.670 --> 00:15:53.445
just automates this for us.

00:15:53.445 --> 00:15:54.820
You'll notice it
uses the G Cloud

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:56.480
command to create the secret.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:58.510
The secret is named
My Secret, and it

00:15:58.510 --> 00:16:00.760
has a value of super
secret, and then

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:02.920
we're granting IAM
permissions on the secret

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:04.660
to our Cloud run
service account,

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:06.295
because secrets are
denied by default,

00:16:06.295 --> 00:16:07.420
so we have to grant access.

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:12.570
Let's go ahead and run
this command to go ahead

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:15.450
and create the
secret, and it looks

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:17.970
like it failed because
I already created it.

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:19.560
Let me fix that real fast.

00:16:23.142 --> 00:16:24.600
This is why you
never do live demo,

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:26.017
something's always
bound to break.

00:16:28.662 --> 00:16:32.430
So I'm just deleting that
in another tab over here.

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:36.090
Let's try that again, we'll go
ahead and create the secret.

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:38.640
And great it succeeded.

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:41.190
You'll also notice that it's
creating version one, that's

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:43.650
because Secret Manager secrets
are automatically versioned,

00:16:43.650 --> 00:16:47.710
but we're just using the
latest here as one example.

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:51.060
And then lastly, we need to
update our deployment script.

00:16:51.060 --> 00:16:55.440
So instead of having this big
long password from Cloud KMS,

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:57.180
we instead just pass
in the reference

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:00.370
to our secret,
which is My Secret.

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:03.550
We'll save this and
deploy the application.

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:06.300
This will need to
rebuild the container,

00:17:06.300 --> 00:17:07.839
because we changed the code.

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:10.140
It'll push it to the
Container Registry,

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:13.502
and then it will
deploy it to Cloud Run,

00:17:13.502 --> 00:17:15.210
and again it will use
the exact same URL,

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:17.125
just creating a new revision.

00:17:17.125 --> 00:17:19.500
So we're just waiting for
traffic to route at this point.

00:17:25.619 --> 00:17:26.670
Great, and it's done.

00:17:26.670 --> 00:17:30.320
And if we jump back over to our
browser and refresh the page,

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:35.300
you'll notice that our Redis
password is still My Secret.

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:38.210
You'll notice that it's now
the reference to my secret,

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:40.125
instead of the
plaintext password.

00:17:40.125 --> 00:17:42.750
And Secret Manager's guarded by
identity and access management,

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:45.680
so our application is retrieving
this value at boot time,

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:48.200
storing the plaintext
only in memory.

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:51.153
And even if this debug
page were to be presented

00:17:51.153 --> 00:17:53.570
an attacker would not be able
to get this plaintext secret

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:56.010
because they don't have
access to Secret Manager.

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:57.770
And we can see that
the application

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:00.020
is totally functional.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:03.750
Great, let's jump back
over to the slides.

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:07.740
So today's journey looked
at three distinct patterns.

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:11.820
Plaintext environment variables,
which no one is going to use,

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:14.130
encrypted environment
variables, and

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:15.810
a centralized secret
storage solution

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:17.970
with Google's Secret Manager.

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:20.580
In the past centralized
secrets management

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:22.500
have required
upfront and continued

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:24.120
operational investment.

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:25.740
With Google's
Secret Manager, you

00:18:25.740 --> 00:18:28.620
have a fully managed
secrets management solution

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:30.760
right on Google Cloud.

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:33.040
We're also expanding
integrations with other Google

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:34.030
Cloud products.

00:18:34.030 --> 00:18:35.740
In the future, you
won't even have

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:38.980
to do this environment variable
dance with Cloud Run or Cloud

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:41.040
Functions or other
Google Cloud products,

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:43.510
because we'll have first
party integrations to directly

00:18:43.510 --> 00:18:46.690
retrieve your secret at runtime.

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:49.170
Now, if you come to earlier
versions of this talk

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.150
you might be familiar
with Berglas.

00:18:51.150 --> 00:18:53.970
If you're not familiar,
Berglas is an open source tool

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:56.430
for doing secret management
on Google Cloud--

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:58.890
it predates Secret
Manager and it largely

00:18:58.890 --> 00:19:00.900
influenced the design
of the first party

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:02.790
product we have today.

00:19:02.790 --> 00:19:06.210
Berglas is a fully interoperable
solution with Google Secret

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:09.960
Manager, we actually designed
it that way from the onset.

00:19:09.960 --> 00:19:12.930
If you're already using Berglas
for your serverless secrets,

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:16.680
migrating Secret
Manager is super easy.

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:20.552
There is a new built in command
in Berglas, Berglas Migrate,

00:19:20.552 --> 00:19:22.260
that takes the name
of the storage bucket

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:25.070
where your secrets are stored,
and automatically migrates

00:19:25.070 --> 00:19:28.380
those from Google Cloud
Storage to Secret Manager,

00:19:28.380 --> 00:19:31.700
keeping their existing
names and permissions.

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:35.330
Then, in your application,
simply change the Berglas

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:38.870
reference to the SM reference--
which is short for Secret

00:19:38.870 --> 00:19:41.250
Manager--

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:43.110
and redeploy your application.

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:44.700
That's actually it.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:46.920
Berglas is completely
interoperable with Secret

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:49.950
Manager, and you don't have to
change your workflow to use it.

00:19:49.950 --> 00:19:51.928
Best of all, you
can mix and match.

00:19:51.928 --> 00:19:53.970
You can have some secrets
that come from Berglas,

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:55.980
and some secrets that
come from Secret Manager,

00:19:55.980 --> 00:19:59.850
all using the same unified API.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:02.670
Thank you so much for
coming on this second Secret

00:20:02.670 --> 00:20:04.383
in Serverless journey with me.

00:20:04.383 --> 00:20:06.550
Hopefully we've made it
clear, the different secrets

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:08.340
management solutions
on Google Cloud,

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:10.720
and how you can best
secure your serverless

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:14.180
secrets in your
serverless applications.

